findings.detail:
MEASURED by decoding build/kernel.bin with t_api_abi's decoder: the kern_big table is 1,259 bytes. It holds 44 SLOT (352), 11 X (77), 112 rare cells (672: RSLOT 50, RCXCELL 24, RCSLOT 19, RNCELL 12, RXCELL 7), 20 inline (108), 6 jump, 1 far, 1 far-jump and 3 fixed refusals. Each rare cell is push bp / call api_r<kind> / dw target, 6 bytes. Packages make about 7,000 source-level call OSAPI_* sites at 5 bytes each.

Proposal, the DOS AH and Mac A-trap model: a rare call becomes 'int VEC / db index' (3 bytes) and the kernel keeps a 2-byte word per rare cell. The kinds are sorted into index ranges at the flag day, so no kind byte is stored. The door:
- fetches the index through the caller's CS:IP and bumps the return IP;
- range-selects the kind;
- runs the existing api_rs/api_rxc/... bodies unchanged;
- returns with retf 2 so CF/ZF answers survive;
- re-enables interrupts only if the caller's IF was set.

An index past this kernel's table is one compare away from CF=1. That closes loader-6's 'newer cell on an older kernel' hole at run time. Hot SLOTs and inline cells are untouched.

Cost: about +80-100 cycles (~20 us) per rare call. Hazards: vector ownership against windowed DOS programs, a kern_mega IDT gate plus a readable code alias, and IF preservation.

kern_big keeps filecp.inc (1,657 .cold + 152 .bss) and fdlg.inc (35 .text + 2,080 .cold + 84 .bss) resident, about 4.0KB. kern_small already ships the same bodies as FILECP.DRV and FDLG.DRV, with resident halves of only about 85+91 .cold, 36 .text and 83 .bss.

ONDEMAND-PLAN 1.2 and 1.3 refused on-demand loading on kern_big because the load needs the system disk at exactly the moment the user's disk is in the drive. That objection applies only to loading on demand. The proposal:
- leave both images in KERNEL.SYS's one contiguous boot read (about 6 packed sectors, roughly +120 ms on a 5150, ESTIMATED);
- copy them into a MEM_PG_LOW claim at mem_unblob;
- pin while entered (ONDEMAND-PLAN 7.1's nesting count, held open-to-close for the dialog and across a copy);
- re-read only after a shed, through the refusal path kern_small already has.

No disk read happens on any machine with heap to spare, and under pressure the heap gets about 4KB back before it refuses a package. KERN_BUDGET counts non-purgeable bytes, so the gauge moves honestly. The same tier would let the existing modules be shed instead of dropped (7.2).

Listing census for kern_big:
- .text->.cold: 27 thunks (6 bytes each) and 63 inline far calls/jumps;
- .cold->.text: 112 far calls (5 bytes each) and 94 near calls into 16 ct_ trampolines;
- about 47 real cw_ shim bodies, 45 of them in kernel.asm's block (plus 23 zero-byte aliases);
- the duplicated kentc/kretc ladder.
As near calls in one segment these would cost about 700 bytes less. kern_small: 12 thunks, 34 inline, 67 far, 67 ct_ calls, about 540 bytes. The targets are spread over 74 and 81 names, the busiest with 13 sites, so per-routine migration is at its floor.

The reason for the split has changed. .text+.bss has 19,842 bytes of KERN_CODE_MAX left. What .cold buys now is movability: the ROM, the HMA (38KB back on a 286) and the slide. On kern_small, .text+.bss+.cold = 54,002 bytes would fit one 64KB segment, so every ROM-less 128KB machine pays about 540 bytes for the ROM option.

CLAUDE.md:5 says both files carry the same guidance. AGENTS.md (786 lines against 1,370) lacks four items that CLAUDE.md has, each confirmed by grep (1 match in CLAUDE.md, 0 in AGENTS.md):
- the RUNGS ARE TEMPORARY banner;
- the 'Design for BYTES, never for rungs' bullet;
- the '.cold IS RESIDENT' paragraph;
- the 'A PACKAGE'S OWN REGION ... RATCHET' bullet.
A Codex agent is therefore never told the rules this lens enforces.
